SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `linkedin_urn`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'Can You Grow Melons Hydroponically in Singapore? Our 11 October 2025 Class Says Yes', 'hydroponics-grow-melons-singapore-class-october-2025', CONCAT('Everyone believes hydroponics grows lettuce. Fewer believe it grows fruit. This rockmelon was grown hydroponically in Singapore ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' no soil, no field, no season. Here is what fruiting crops demand that leafy greens do not, and what our 11 October 2025 WSQ class covered.'), '<p>Ask most people what hydroponics grows and they will say lettuce. They are not wrong &mdash; leafy greens are the easiest, fastest and most forgiving crop in a soil-less system, which is why every beginner starts there and why most commercial vertical farms are stacked with them.</p> <p>But it is a ceiling people impose on the technology rather than one the technology has. Hydroponics grows fruit. Including, in Singapore, melons.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2025-10-11-hydroponic-rockmelon.jpg" alt="A ripe rockmelon grown hydroponically in Singapore, cut in half to show dense orange flesh and a seed cavity" /> <figcaption>A rockmelon grown hydroponically in Singapore &mdash; no soil, no field, no growing season. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>That is a rockmelon. It was grown in a nutrient solution, not soil, in a country with no melon season and effectively no farmland. We showed it in our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html">WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</a> class on <strong>11 October 2025</strong> for exactly that reason: it resets what people think the method is for.</p> <h2>Why melons are the interesting case</h2> <p>A lettuce is a leaf. You give it light, water, nutrients and a few weeks, and you harvest the whole plant. A melon is a fruit, which means the plant has to complete a much longer and more demanding programme: grow a vine, flower, get pollinated, set fruit, and then pump weeks of sugar into it.</p> <p>Each of those stages asks something a leafy green never does:</p> <ul> <li><strong>The nutrient recipe has to change with the stage.</strong> Vegetative growth wants nitrogen. Fruiting wants relatively more potassium and phosphorus. A single fixed formula that grows beautiful leaves will give you a vine with disappointing fruit.</li> <li><strong>EC generally rises as the fruit develops.</strong> Growers commonly push conductivity higher during fruiting, which is one of the levers behind sweetness &mdash; and precisely why the meter is not optional.</li> <li><strong>Pollination will not happen by itself indoors.</strong> No wind, no bees. Melon flowers get pollinated by hand, usually with a small brush, and the timing matters because the female flower is receptive only briefly.</li> <li><strong>The fruit needs physical support.</strong> A developing melon is heavy enough to tear itself off the vine. Growers sling each fruit in a net.</li> <li><strong>You must thin the fruit.</strong> A vine that sets six melons ripens six mediocre ones. Cut it back to one or two and the plant concentrates its sugar where you want it.</li> <li><strong>It takes months, not weeks.</strong> Every mistake has a long feedback loop, which is why people practise on lettuce first.</li> </ul> <p>The reward for that work is a fruit grown with no soil, no field, no season and no pesticide regime, in a system whose conditions you set. The <a href="https://www.sfa.gov.sg/food-for-thought/article/detail/hydroponics-getting-to-the-root-of-the-myths">Singapore Food Agency</a> makes the general version of this point: control over temperature, humidity, airflow and CO2 lets a grower deliberately produce a desired taste and texture profile. With a melon, that control shows up directly as sweetness.</p> <h2>Everything starts the same way</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2025-10-11-seedlings-net-pots.jpg" alt="Young lettuce and leafy vegetable seedlings growing in net pots filled with clay pebbles under an LED grow light, each pot hand-labelled" /> <figcaption>Labelled seedlings in net pots of clay pebbles under LED. Melon or lettuce, the first weeks look identical. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>This is where a melon and a lettuce are indistinguishable: a seedling in a net pot of clay pebbles, roots reaching for the solution, under a light. The clay pebbles hold no nutrition at all &mdash; they exist to anchor the plant and hold air around the roots, because roots need oxygen as much as they need water. Drowned roots rot; that is the most common way a beginner''s system dies.</p> <p>The hand-written labels are not decoration either. When several varieties share a tray at the same stage, the label is the only thing that tells you which is which &mdash; and knowing which cultivar is in front of you is the difference between a record and a guess.</p> <h2>The same principles, at commercial scale</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2025-10-11-rooftop-nft-learning-journey.jpg" alt="Class participants on a rooftop learning journey inspecting solar-powered NFT hydroponic grow beds under netted enclosures at Singapore Polytechnic" /> <figcaption>Learning journey to a solar-powered rooftop NFT installation &mdash; the same principles as a tabletop kit, on a car-park deck. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>This is the argument for hydroponics in Singapore, in one photograph: a working farm on a rooftop deck, growing food on a surface that already existed and produces nothing otherwise. The white PVC channels are <strong>nutrient film technique</strong> &mdash; a thin, continuous film of nutrient solution flowing past bare roots, recirculating back to the reservoir rather than draining away. The panels overhead run the pumps. The netting keeps pests out without pesticide.</p> <p>Nothing there is conceptually different from the kit on the classroom bench. Same solution, same measurements, same principles &mdash; larger channels and a pump with more work to do. That is the point of a learning journey: it makes the jump from tabletop to production feel like a change of scale rather than a change of subject.</p> <p>Singapore is full of this pattern. <a href="https://www.sfa.gov.sg/fromSGtoSG/farms/farm/Detail/comcrop">ComCrop</a> grows on a Woodlands rooftop. <a href="https://www.sfa.gov.sg/fromSGtoSG/farms/farm/Detail/sustenir-agriculture">Sustenir</a> grows strawberries and kale indoors in a fully controlled climate. An <a href="https://enterprise.nus.edu.sg/startup-story/singrow-2/">NUS spin-off, Singrow</a>, has worked on cultivars bred for tropical controlled-environment growing. And in 2025 <a href="https://www.businesstimes.com.sg/singapore/smes/singapore-food-output-gets-s80-million-boost-opening-greenphytos-vertical-farm-tallest-world">Greenphyto opened a vertical farm</a> with 23-metre growing chambers, reported as the tallest in the world. None of it needs farmland.</p> <h2>What the class covers</h2> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html">WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</a> (course code TGS-2025053916) is one day, 8 hours, Beginner level, with a 1-hour assessment, held at <strong>MEOD Farm, 13 Neo Tiew Harvest Lane</strong>. It maps to the SkillsFuture Skills Framework TSC <strong>Urban Farming Implementation and Management Control (AGR-FOP-2002-1.1)</strong>, with two learning outcomes:</p> <ol> <li>Perform farming activities and equipment operations according to standard operating procedures</li> <li>Execute farming operations work in accordance with regulations, WSH practices and housekeeping rules</li> </ol> <p>Across the day you cover hydroponic system types (deep water culture, NFT, wick, ebb and flow, drip), growing media, plant nutrition and nutrient solutions, pH and EC monitoring, indoor farming conditions including lighting and humidity, Singapore''s urban farming regulations, good agriculture practices and workplace safety &mdash; plus a guided tour of a commercial hydroponics farm and hands-on practice with a hydroponics kit.</p> <p>Melons are not a syllabus item. What the course gives you is the foundation that makes attempting one sensible: if you can hold a stable pH and EC, keep roots oxygenated and read what a plant is telling you, adapting to a fruiting crop is a matter of adjusting the recipe and adding hand pollination, netting and thinning. Try it without that foundation and you will lose several months finding out.</p> <h3>What it costs</h3> <p>The full fee is <strong>$350</strong> before GST. With WSQ funding:</p> <table> <tr><th>Who you are</th><th>Nett fee</th><th>Funding</th></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen / PR, aged 21 and above</td><td><strong>$206.50</strong></td><td>50% course-fee funding</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored</td><td><strong>$136.50</strong></td><td>70% course-fee funding</td></tr> </table> <p>GST of $31.50 (9%) applies to the full course fee. Funding is valid <strong>from 24 March 2025 to 23 March 2027</strong>. Eligible Singapore Citizens can apply <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong> and <strong>PSEA</strong> against the nett fee, NTUC members may claim <strong>UTAP</strong>, and employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> with <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong> support. Completion &mdash; 75% attendance plus a pass &mdash; earns a Certificate of Achievement from Tertiary Infotech Academy and an <strong>OpenCert from SkillsFuture Singapore</strong>.</p> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>Check the next class date and funding eligibility &rarr;</strong></a></p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does the course teach me to grow melons specifically?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No &mdash; it teaches the fundamentals that fruiting crops are built on: system types, nutrient solutions, pH and EC control, growing media, and indoor environment management. Melons are shown as proof of what the method can do. Growing one is a project you take on afterwards, with the measurement discipline the course gives you.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is realistically the easiest thing to grow first?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Leafy greens and herbs &mdash; lettuce, bok choy, kailan, basil, mint. They are fast, forgiving, and harvestable in weeks, so you get through several complete cycles (and several mistakes) in the time a single melon would take.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Why do hydroponic plants sit in clay pebbles if there is no nutrition in them?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The medium''s job is structural, not nutritional. Clay pebbles anchor the plant and hold air pockets around the roots, which need oxygen as well as water &mdash; permanently submerged roots rot. All the nutrition arrives dissolved in the solution. Rockwool and coco peat do the same job with different water-retention behaviour.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need a big space or a rooftop?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. A shelf with an LED grow light and a small reservoir is enough for a household supply of greens. The rooftop farms show what the same principles look like scaled up &mdash; useful if you are thinking commercially, but not a prerequisite for starting.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is this course funded, and how long is the funding valid?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. WSQ funding brings the $350 fee to $206.50 for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above, or $136.50 for Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above under MCES. SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and UTAP can be applied on top. Funding runs from 24 March 2025 to 23 March 2027.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>The melon is a demonstration, not a target. The real point is that in a country importing over 90% of its food on almost no farmland, growing anything at all without soil is a capability worth having &mdash; and the people who have it started by learning to mix a solution and read a meter.</p> <ol> <li>Start with leafy greens and run at least two full crop cycles before attempting fruit.</li> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</strong></a> class &mdash; from <strong>$136.50 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above.</li> <li>Keep a log of pH, EC and what you changed. It is the only way the long feedback loop of a fruiting crop becomes learnable.</li> </ol> <p>New to all this? Start with our companion post on <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-singapore-urban-farming-class-june-2026">why hydroponics matters for Singapore</a>, from the 6 June 2026 run of the same course.</p>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2025-10-11', 'TGS-2025053916', 'TGS-2025053916', 'Growing Melons Hydroponically in Singapore | WSQ Urban Farming Course', 'Hydroponics is not just lettuce &mdash; this rockmelon was grown in a nutrient solution in Singapore. What fruiting crops need that leafy greens do not, from our 11 October 2025 WSQ urban farming class. Funded from $136.50 nett.', 'hydroponic melons, grow melons hydroponically, hydroponics singapore, rockmelon hydroponics, urban farming singapore, wsq hydroponics course, nutrient film technique, hand pollination melon, vertical farming singapore, skillsfuture urban farming', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'hydroponics-grow-melons-singapore-class-october-2025') x);

